# Tamil Nadu District Municipalities (Amendment) Bill: Restoring Dissolved Municipal Councils
Tamil title: மாவட்ட நகராட்சி (திருத்த) சட்ட முன்வடிவு

Speech delivered in the Legislative Assembly on 28 March 1967 on Tamil Nadu District Municipalities (Amendment) Bill No. 2 of 1967.

Mr Speaker, the amendment now placed before the House has been introduced to correct an injustice already committed. Its purpose is not to stain the previous administration, but to wipe away the stain its own action left upon it.

The Tirunelveli Municipal Council and several other municipal councils were dissolved on the ground that they could not perform their duties properly. When that occurred, DMK members then seated in Opposition, along with members of other parties, argued that it gravely injured democracy, prevented local bodies from living, and sought to extend political domination into every sphere. They said it was a wrongful course.

They also pointed out that municipalities guilty of more serious conduct were left alone because they supported the ruling party. Karunanidhi raised those examples in this House. The Government not only failed to take them to heart but appealed after the Tirunelveli municipality brought a case and initially obtained a ruling that its dissolution was wrong.

Ponnappa Nadar now asks whether enacting a new law after the High Court's final decision, in order to depart from or escape it, would turn justice aside. We are prepared to bow to the High Court's judgment. But none of us should forget that the final court is the public. Dr Hande spoke movingly of the verdict delivered by that final court. Since elected representatives sit in this Assembly, it acts on behalf of the public as the last court in the political sphere.

This Bill is therefore introduced to provide justice, not to escape judgments already delivered.

For too long we have held a mistaken idea that every judgment is itself justice. If judgment and justice were identical, every court would reach the same judgment, and decisions made fifty or sixty years apart could never differ. Justice is enduring; a judgment is the conclusion a court reaches from the information placed before it at a particular time. We should not confuse ourselves into believing that one judgment exhausts all justice beyond it.

There is thus no contradiction between this Bill and the High Court's decision, nor are we transgressing that decision.

Another member asked whether we had resolved to change everything done by the former Government. Two days ago, when we said we were continuing something it had done, members asked whether we must imitate it in everything. This only shows that my friends are somewhat bewildered about which accusation to make.

We must know what should and should not be changed. When we alter something, do not ask merely whether the previous Government did it; ask whether it deserves alteration. Forget for a moment that the DMK controlled Tirunelveli municipality and that a Congress administration dissolved it. Examine only whether adequate grounds existed.

If it is difficult to decide while seated here facing me, examine the allegations later in the afternoon. A municipality might justly be dissolved if it obstructed essential statutory duties such as public health, drainage, sanitation or other core civic functions. The printed English list of duties is damaged by Tamil OCR, but the principle is clear.

The principal allegation against Tirunelveli was that its meetings were disorderly. Many lawyers sit on both sides of this House. If a municipality could survive only when every meeting concluded without disturbance, there would be no reason for municipal law to prescribe what a chairperson may do when disruption occurs.

The law empowers a chairperson to remove members who deliberately disrupt meetings and even to suspend them from three or four sittings. Its authors therefore anticipated disturbances. Ours is a warm country and emotions rise quickly. In colder lands they may rise less readily. Here disturbances occur not only in municipalities but even in Parliament. If Congress doctrine required dissolving every body in which disorder occurs, Parliament would have to be dissolved six times a month.

The allegation of disorder may have been argued in court, but its merits must be examined here. Did anyone have to be taken to a magistrate's court or hospital? Nothing of the sort. After proceedings ended, members put arms around one another's shoulders, compared what each had said, and went home peacefully.

During the stated period, fifty-three meetings took place. The Government alleged disorder in seven. If a Congress Government containing respected friends decided to dissolve the municipality because seven of fifty-three meetings were disorderly, the decision would remain a lasting reproach. This Bill comes from a friend's duty to wipe away that reproach, not from a petty desire to enjoy seeing former councillors restored after six months.

Several former municipal representatives now sit here in this Assembly itself. The Bill does not serve our personal gratification. Imagine future generations reading why Tirunelveli municipality was dissolved. Imagine a future law student, or even a descendant of those responsible, discovering that such an injustice occurred while everyone kept silent. We seek to erase that injustice, not merely to gather the old council for our pleasure.

Our friend Martin questioned Dr Hande's reference to strictures. I understand Martin is a lawyer and hope he succeeds greatly in that profession. He said the High Court had passed no separate document called "strictures." Of course a judgment is not divided into one document labelled judgment and another labelled strictures. Lawyers and non-lawyers alike must use reason to distinguish the operative ruling from critical observations.

Dr Hande read the judgment rationally and found many strictures. I will not weary the House by reading them all. Another member said this Bill contradicts the High Court. On the contrary, as I first explained, it follows the basis of the Court's observations so that blame does not remain upon the Congress administration.

There were two Tirunelveli cases, one in the lower court and another on appeal. Anyone wishing to study the matter in detail may read Justice Kailasam's judgment, which examined whether sufficient grounds existed for dissolution. The Government's appeal did not ask whether sufficient grounds existed; it asked whether the Government possessed the legal power and had acted under the relevant statutory section.

The Chief Justice was required to decide that limited question. Read together, the two judgments give the full history. Justice Kailasam held that sufficient grounds for dissolution did not exist. On appeal, the Chief Justice held that the appellate court need not decide sufficiency, only whether the Government possessed authority. The ruling affirmed that authority, but also indicated that, if asked whether adequate grounds existed, the answer would be no. Dr Hande called that latter portion the strictures.

Chief Justice Anantanarayanan could have ended by saying simply that the Government possessed statutory power and nothing further need be stated. Instead, he deliberately added observations. The original English passages quoted across scan pages eight to ten are severely corrupted by Tamil OCR. Anna's immediately following Tamil explanation, however, makes their thrust clear.

The Court said in substance that it lacked authority in that proceeding to substitute its judgment on the merits, but that if it had such authority the Government's appeal ought to fail. It questioned whether the dissolution was wise or justified and used words severe enough to make those responsible feel pain and shame. Anna likened the Government's claim of victory to striking one cheek and then offering the other merely because it remained untouched.

The Court further observed that it was not yet too late for the Government to reconsider whether the action was justified and conducive to public welfare. “Government” does not mean only the Congress Government; government is continuous. The present Government therefore accepts the judicial advice and reconsiders the matter. The Bill demonstrates to the world that a Government may sometimes act wrongly and can correct itself.

The Court advised taking time to reconsider both the public interest and the procedural course. When the High Court itself has effectively said, "You have the authority; use the opportunity to erase this injustice," a Government committed to justice should follow that advice. That goodwill is why the Bill has been introduced.

To summarise: first, the Bill corrects a wrong already done; second, it removes the reproach attached to the Congress administration; third, it accepts the Court's broader principle that government must act upon justice. No reasons greater than these are needed. I ask the House to approach the Bill in that spirit.

**R. Ponnappa Nadar:** Mr Speaker, will the Chief Minister say when the next municipal elections will be held?

**C. N. Annadurai:** That question may have two purposes. One may simply wish to know the date. Or one may ask why this Bill is needed if elections will come soon. Since no decision has yet been made on how soon the next municipal elections will take place, we consider the Bill necessary now.

---NOTES---
The OCR line identifying the enactment contains a damaged parenthetical date and number. The printed subject and surrounding text clearly identify the Tamil Nadu District Municipalities (Amendment) Bill No. 2 of 1967, debated on 28 March 1967.

Several lengthy High Court quotations printed in English were processed with Tamil OCR and are not recoverable reliably from this Markdown. Their legal effect is translated from Anna's adjacent Tamil paraphrase; the quotations themselves require English re-OCR from images 8-10.

The source alternates between sections 41(1) and 47(1) when discussing statutory power. Both readings are preserved in the Tamil source and require checking against the Act and scans before legal citation.

The distinction among the operative judgment, judicial observations and “strictures” is rendered according to Anna's own explanation, not presented as modern legal advice.
